<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">Aspendos</h1><p class="st-meta">650 BCE – 25 BCE (approx.) · capital Aspendos</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Aspendos, a wealthy Pamphylian city on the Eurymedon river, near which Athens defeated Persia and later the Romans built a famous theatre.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Aspendos rose on a hill by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eurymedon-river">Eurymedon</a> river, a few kilometres from the sea, in the 7th century BCE. Its people claimed descent from settlers from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/argos">Argos</a>, but its coins were inscribed in the local Pamphylian Greek dialect with the name Estwediiys.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The city prospered from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horse-breeding">horse breeding</a>, farming and salt and river trade, and minted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> coins. Around 466 BCE the Athenian general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cimon">Cimon</a> defeated a Persian fleet and army near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eurymedon-river">Eurymedon</a>. In 333 BCE Aspendos submitted to Alexander, who demanded tribute and horses after it reneged on terms.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Aspendos passed under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucid</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pergamenes">Pergamene</a> influence, then Roman rule, joining the province of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/galatia">Galatia</a> in 25 BCE. Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a>, built in the 2nd century CE, is one of the best preserved from antiquity.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/aspendos.jpg" alt="Aspendos" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Dosseman · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aspendos_Basilica_4728.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>650 BCE – 25 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/7th-century-bce">7th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Aspendos</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Greek religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Pamphylian Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Aspendus</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-338&amp;focus=aspendos" aria-label="Aspendos on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5462 847 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Aspendos lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5782 1103L5591 1068M5782 1103L6140 1251M5782 1103L5304 942"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5591 1068h0M6140 1251h0M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5782 1103h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5782 1103h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-338&amp;focus=aspendos">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

